WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.359
Hello and welcome to the domain of science, Dominic here...

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.240
And this week is a very exciting week because we can now experience a landmark event in the history of space travel.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:15.330
If all goes to plan on the 27th of May 2020, we're going to see for the first time in nine years

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:20.369
the launch of astronauts from American soil going to the International Space Station.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:26.070
The last mission of this kind was the final NASA space shuttle mission in 2011 before the Space Shuttle was

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.890
decommissioned, after which all of the missions to the space station were launched from Russia on Soyuz Rockets.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.889
And this new mission is also a big deal because it's the first crewed mission to the International Space Station to be run

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:39.250
by a private company.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:46.020
SpaceX aren't merely supplying the rocket and spacecraft to NASA, they're actually running the entire mission as well.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.960
So this really is the beginning of commercial human spaceflight.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.060
In this video I've tried to summarize everything you need to know about this mission.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:00.400
This mission is the final test to validate that SpaceX can deliver astronauts to and from the space station.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.250
The astronauts flying this mission are Douglas G. Hurley and Robert L. Behnken –

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.599
they're both veterans of the space shuttle program with two flights each under their belts

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.580
and it's very cool for Douglas Hurley because he was the pilot of the final space shuttle mission and now

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.360
he's the pilot of the first commercial flight.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:23.980
They've both come across as being very calm and confident, which is what you want – if it was me, I would be feeling very anxious.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.480
Obviously, everyone's gone to great lengths to make sure everything's as safe as possible, but

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:37.700
spaceflight is inherently risky and being the first people to sit on top of a new rocket and get blasted into space is –

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:44.420
Well, I can't even imagine what that would be like. Uh, probably pretty amazing and pretty terrifying.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.860
This is the vehicle they'll be traveling in. Most of this is the Falcon 9 rocket –

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:53.260
that'll get them into space. But the top bit is the spacecraft housing the astronauts.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.860
For safety this is a kind of...

00:01:55.869 --> 00:02:00.839
ejector mechanism that can launch it away from the main rocket if there are any problems during the launch.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.299
This is called the Integrated Launch Escape System.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:11.669
And everything on this spacecraft has been slightly over-engineered in case of failures: the escape system only really needs four engines,

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.650
but it's got eight for redundancy.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.640
And the main Falcon 9 rocket

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.190
only needs seven engines to get into space, but has got nine just in case.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.109
On this test flight only two seats are going to be used, but it's got capacity for seven astronauts.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.360
The biggest change from other spacecraft is that this one's controlled via touchscreen monitors.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:37.980
I have to admit, I was kind of surprised when I learned this. I would have thought that a single point of failure would be

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.500
risky from an engineering point of view.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:46.260
All of your controls go through one screen and you can't really fix if it breaks.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.400
But what do I know? I'm not a space engineer. I'm sure they've tested this like crazy and on the plus side

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:57.510
it does free up a lot of space from [the] million switches and levers that you see in other spacecraft.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.329
And anyway, most of the flight is completely

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:05.880
automated. Although, the astronauts do have a manual override if they need to take control.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.790
Let's take a look at the overall mission:

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.600
Another difference to other missions is that the astronauts will be sitting on top of the rocket while it's being fueled.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.200
In other missions they fuel the rocket before the astronauts embark.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.940
They're fueling it with the astronauts on top so that they can launch as soon as the fuel is in the rocket,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:30.120
which they want to do because they're using cooled propellants – liquid oxygen and kerosene – which are more effective when they're cold.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.219
The worry is that the rocket could explode during fueling, which has happened once but

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:41.610
SpaceX have fueled safely many times and NASA have given it the green light, so they think it's safe. So fingers crossed

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.390
everything will be okay

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:47.909
After the launch, the first stage of the rocket separates and comes back to earth to land on a barge,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:52.649
which is an amazing feat of engineering because this part of the rocket is reusable –

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:55.940
It brings the cost of spaceflight way down for this mission.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:02.200
On the way to the space station the astronauts will test fly the spacecraft with the manual controls and then

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.660
oversee an automatic docking with the space station

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.340
Then they'll be working on the space station for two to three months, before flying back down to earth –

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.800
I say flying. It's more of a ...controlled falling.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:21.400
This mission is the final test to prove to NASA that SpaceX can safely deliver astronauts to and from the International Space Station.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:25.500
They've made lots of cargo deliveries before, but never transported people.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:30.200
And to get to this stage, they've had to pass a whole set of safety tests shown here:

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.920
the pad abort test was to test the escape system to make sure it works properly and this was passed in 2015, but

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:43.760
unfortunately in 2019 the escape system rockets exploded during a test, which set the whole schedule back by nearly a year.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.820
Fortunately, they fixed the issue at the end of 2019

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.980
Retested it and all works okay, and then they

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:57.560
successfully tested the escape system in midair where they blew up a whole rocket to make sure that the crew capsule could get away from it okay.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:03.620
Back in March of 2019 they ran this whole mission to the space station and back, just without the astronauts on board.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:11.180
And so this mission, scheduled for the 27th of May, will be the final test to prove that they can safely deliver people to space.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.040
If this all works, it's very cool because it's a new,

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:21.640
cheaper way to get people into space. And this has all happened because of NASA's Commercial Crew program.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:29.600
This is a NASA program to fund commercial companies to incentivize them to work out ways of efficiently getting into space.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:35.240
And SpaceX is not the only beneficiary of this program, Boeing also has a spacecraft in the works as well.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:43.100
And so this mission, if successful, really marks the beginning of commercial space travel and potentially the beginning of space tourism.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:49.240
In the future a company called Space Adventures is planning to sell tickets to fly into space beyond the Space Station

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.920
for something like 50 million dollars a ticket.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.650
This is... a little bit out of my price range

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:56.840
but for some people – some very wealthy people –

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:04.180
that'll be an amazing experience that they can buy. And that ticket price is way cheaper than any seat to space in the past.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:10.460
NASA have also given the go-ahead to a company called Axiom Space, who are planning to build a commercial space station in orbit

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:14.000
which would also be served by rockets like the SpaceX Falcon 9.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:17.740
But all of these plans hinge on the success of the mission this week

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:23.779
which is why it feels like a landmark event in space aviation. I'm very excited to watch it,

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.440
I'll be there watching it as it happens, but I want to hear from you, too.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.340
Will you watching and what do you think about this commercialization of space and space tourism?

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.720
Do you think it's a good way to fund space science, or are you generally

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.480
worried about what all these wealthy people will be doing up there? [chuckle]

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.840
Leave me your thoughts in the comments below and if you've enjoyed this video,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:48.599
feel free to check out some of my others. And I just want to say a special thanks to my Patreon supporters

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.420
who make videos like this possible. Thank you.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:56.680
All right, see you on the 27th. Hopefully everything goes amazingly well.

